/* ============================================================================
   population-stdev.epher -- standard deviation of the ten scores, dividing by n

   The population standard deviation of the exam scores in `d`, from the
   builtin stdev and cross-checked as the square root of the population
   variance. epher's stdev is the POPULATION version: variance divides by
   the count n, and stdev is its square root (guide 1.20). When the data
   are a sample, use the n - 1 version (sample-stdev.epher shows it).

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/statistics/summary/population-stdev.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\statistics\summary\population-stdev.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/statistics/summary/population-stdev.epher
   In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
   Everything also works line by line in the TUI.

   The one knob to edit is `d` below. Everything above the results section
   is machinery you can leave alone.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob -----------------------------------------------------------------
const d = {72, 85, 91, 64, 88, 79, 83, 95, 70, 86}   // exam scores, 10 students

// ---- population standard deviation: sqrt of the population variance -------------
stdev(d)                        // builtin stdev, population version (n)
sqrt(variance(d))               // sqrt of the builtin variance, agrees
variance(d)                     // the variance it comes from

/* ---- expected output ----
= {72, 85, 91, 64, 88, 79, 83, 95, 70, 86}
= 9.40265919833
= 9.40265919833
= 88.41
*/